    {
      "title": "North Korean hackers behind $6.3 billion Binance crypto heist, says FBI",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/north-korean-hackers-behind-6-3-billion-binance-crypto-heist-says-fbi-126110100038_1.html",
      "description": "The theft of billions of dollars from Binance within a matter of minutes, the biggest crypto heist in history, was orchestrated by North Korean hackers, the FBI has said.\nThe bureau said in a statement on Saturday that this week's attack was tied to \"malicious cyber activity\" by an entity known as TraderTraitor, the same actors behind a USD 1.5 billion Bybit hack last year.\nThe FBI asked crypto exchanges, blockchain analytics companies and others to block transactions linked to various addresses to make sure the money is not laundered to cover up its origins.\nIt also said its probe found that TraderTraitor was converting stolen assets primarily into a cryptocurrency called ethereum. The FBI expects that TraderTraitor will now attempt to convert the assets using DeFi and \"instant swap services.\"\nOn Thursday, USD 6.3 billion in bitcoin and other digital currency was stolen from Binance, the world's largest crypto exchange, in what several crypto experts said bore the hallmarks of a .",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-01T02:17:27"
    },
    {
      "title": "Binance CEO says 90 percent of stolen assets have been ‘replenished’",
      "link": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1024091/binance-ceo-90-percent-stolen-crypto",
      "description": "After hackers stole a shocking $6.3 billion from Binance customers in a record-breaking crypto heist, the company's CEO says that 90 percent of the funds have been replaced, and the company plans to cover the remaining 10 percent as well. Binance is covering the stolen funds directly, and the remaining 10 percent consists of \"less [&#8230;]",
      "source_name": "The Verge All Posts",
